"1. Load the MovieLens100K ratings from the local `u.data` file in the current working directory using the correct tab-separated schema: user, item, rating, timestamp.\n2. Use LensKit\u2019s ImplicitMF recommender as the only model under study, with one fixed set of hyperparameters reused for every run.\n3. Create a single train/test split procedure that is explicitly defined and reproducible; keep the data split fixed across seed trials so model-seed effects are isolated from split randomness.\n4. Convert the explicit ratings data into the implicit-feedback format expected by the experiment, and clearly define the relevance criterion used for evaluation.\n5. Run the model training and recommendation pipeline multiple times with identical settings except for the random seed passed to the model/training process.\n6. Use enough distinct seeds/runs to estimate variability meaningfully (e.g., at least 10 runs).\n7. Generate top-N recommendations for each test user from each trained model, excluding items already seen in that user\u2019s training data.\n8. Evaluate each run with ranking metrics appropriate to the task, including at least one of NDCG@K and Recall@K, using the same cutoff K for all runs.\n9. Aggregate metric values across users within each run, then summarize variability across runs with statistics such as mean, standard deviation, min, max, and/or confidence intervals.\n10. Report per-seed results in a table or dataframe and produce a concise analysis quantifying how much metrics change across seeds.\n11. Include a direct comparison showing whether between-seed variation is practically important, such as relative standard deviation or metric range as a percentage of the mean.\n12. Ensure all random number generators used by the model/training pipeline are explicitly seeded so runs are actually controlled and reproducible.\n13. Handle users with no eligible test relevance data correctly during evaluation to avoid invalid metric calculations.\n14. Implement the experiment as a runnable script or notebook that executes end-to-end without manual intervention, assuming `u.data` is present and required Python packages are installed.\n15. Print or save the final summary and a short conclusion on whether reporting random seeds appears important for this LensKit ImplicitMF setup."